From 3534d03dd92803e185861dffd41da13e483375b4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: lucascouts <lucascs@protonmail.com>
Date: Sun, 4 Oct 2026 09:37:27 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] feat(agent_servers): reopen a thread in the config it was
 left in

Reopening an ACP thread -- session/load or session/resume, after a restart or
from the history -- ran the same step a brand-new thread runs:
apply_default_config_options sent every entry default in the agent's
default_config_options, unconditionally, once the agent had answered. A thread
left on Plan with a non-default model came back on the entry's mode and model,
and the plus adapter's own restoration of the mode (0.23.5) was overwritten a
moment after it happened. Nothing on screen said so.

Option A, skipping the defaults on load, was rejected: an agent that restores
nothing itself -- Codex, and most others -- would then reopen on the agent's
bare defaults instead of the entry's, which is the opposite regression. So the
client remembers, per thread, the last state it saw, and prefers it.

Store. One JSON record per (agent id, session id) in the key-value store
upstream already ships (db::kvp::KeyValueStore, namespace acp_thread_config),
the way agent_ui keeps unsent prompts. The key is the JSON array
[agent_id, session_id], never a concatenation: an agent id is free text and a
separator inside it must not make two threads share a record. No table and no
migration is added, so neither an upgrade nor a downgrade can meet a migration
this patch wrote, and refresh.sh has no positional migration array to
collide with -- an older release simply never reads the namespace.

Recorder. One task per live session wakes on the config-options watch that
already fires on every accepted change (a user pick, an agent
ConfigOptionUpdate, the response set_config_option adopts) and on a new mode
trigger, which set_mode, the CurrentModeUpdate branch and new_session's mode
default now fire. It snapshots the cells as they are when it wakes -- the
watch coalesces -- and writes. It never reads a cell right after an optimistic
write: a rejected pick changes nothing and signals nothing, and every revert
path now signals, so a snapshot taken while a rejection was pending is
replaced by the next one. Writes are detached, so closing a session right
after a change does not lose it, and chained, so two writes started close
together cannot land out of order.

Restoration. apply_default_config_options becomes
apply_session_config_options and takes the record, which open_or_create_session
reads before sending session/load, because an agent may replay updates during
the load. Per option, a pure choose_value decides: a recorded value the agent
offers and already reports is skipped (no request for an unchanged value); one
it offers and does not report is sent; one it no longer offers is dropped with
a warning naming the option, the value, the agent and the session, and the
entry default applies, exactly as before. With no record the behaviour is the
old one. A legacy-mode agent also gets its recorded mode back. new_session
passes no record, so a new or forked thread starts from the defaults. The
restoration runs inside upstream's sequential defaults loop (#65233): each pass
re-reads the options the agent last returned, so a recorded reasoning effort
lands after the recorded model that offers it, and the drop warning is emitted
once per option rather than once per pass. Only the value's source is new.

Delete. ThreadMetadataStore::delete removes the thread's record beside its
draft prompt, so no record outlives its thread; delete_all goes through it.

Generic ACP, no adapter key read, but it changes what the plus adapter's
threads do on every reopen and was proved there, so it rides
claude-agent-acp-plus BY CHOICE, like 0010. Proved in a built Zeo: a
Plus thread on Plan and Sonnet 5.5, restarted and reopened, came back on
Auto and the default model without this patch and on Plan and Sonnet 5.5
with it.

Tests added (0.2.3_p20261009): the recorded mode is restored through session/resume as
through session/load (the test agent now offers resume), and two rapid accepted
picks record the last one.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
